>
  <h2>What "cheapest" usually leaves out</h2>
  <p>
    A virtual office address has two jobs. One is the business address you put on your website, invoices and letters. The other is the
    registered office. That's the official address Companies House holds for your company. Many of the cheapest prices cover the first job only.
    Before you compare monthly prices, check which of these are in the price and which are extra:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The registered office address.</strong> It's often an extra, or a higher tier. The cheap price may be a "trading address" only. You can't use that for Companies House or a business bank account.</li>
    <li><strong>A director's service address.</strong> The address shown against you as a director. Without it, your home can stay on the public register, even after the company's registered office changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Post.</strong> Some plans only take in official post from Companies House and HMRC. Forwarding other post costs extra for each item. Scanning is on a higher plan.</li>
    <li><strong>A setup fee.</strong> A one-off charge of £20 to £50 is common. It's rarely in the headline price.</li>
    <li><strong>An identity check fee.</strong> Providers must do anti-money laundering checks. A few charge for them.</li>
    <li><strong>VAT.</strong> "From £10" is sometimes £12 once VAT is added. Check if the price shown includes it.</li>
    <li><strong>The contract.</strong> The lowest monthly rate is often a year paid in advance. A monthly rolling plan can cost a little more each month. But it costs much less if you only need it for six months.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a cheap address must still be</h2>
  <p>Whatever the price, Companies House has the same rules for a registered office:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>An appropriate address.</strong> Post sent to the company would reach someone acting for it, and they could acknowledge it.</li>
    <li><strong>A physical address, not a PO box.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>In the right part of the UK.</strong> A company registered in England and Wales needs a registered office in England or Wales. Scottish and Northern Irish companies need one in Scotland or Northern Ireland.</li>
    <li><strong>With the provider's permission.</strong> You get this with your agreement and their anti-money laundering checks on you.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A building with staff, where real people take in the post, meets all four. Use this test on any price that looks too good.
    Who, exactly, picks up the letter from HMRC?
  </p>

