    {' '}{formatPrice(withCover(p20))} a month. The {formatPrice(p40.price)} {p40.name} plan adds unlimited post scanning and two free meeting
    room hours a month, worth {formatPrice(p40.price)}.
  </p>

  <h2>Paying for perks you will never use</h2>
  <p>
    The big-brand virtual offices start at £65 a month in Liverpool. There's a reason for the price. It includes things a small business
    outside the city centre doesn't need. Part of what you pay is for lounges and buildings in other cities. A plumber in
    Prescot or a care company in Kirkby will never set foot in them. Part of it is the postcode. A city-centre address doesn't make a
    Knowsley business easier to find. And when a client does want to meet, you pay to park. The brand name costs money too.
  </p>
  <p>
    For most small businesses in Merseyside, the real test is simpler. Is it a real building with staff? Will someone you can name
    tell you when your post arrives? Can you get there and park for free when you need to? Is there a room you can meet a client in?
    That's what the price should buy. It's what the {formatPrice(p20.price)} and {formatPrice(p40.price)} plans at the Maggie
    O'Neill Centre are built around.
  </p>

  <h2>How to compare prices properly</h2>
  <p>Ask every provider the same six questions. Write the answers for each one in its own row. At the end, the cheapest row is rarely the cheapest headline.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Is the registered office address included, or a separate fee?</li>
    <li>Is a director's service address included? What does it add to the price?</li>
    <li>What happens to post? Is it collected, forwarded for a fee per item, or scanned? What does a scanned letter cost?</li>
    <li>Is there a setup fee, an identity check fee, or VAT on top?</li>
    <li>What is the minimum term? Can you cancel monthly?</li>
    <li>Is it a building with staff that you could visit? Or is it a mailbox at a sorting address?</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>When the cheapest is not the best value</h2>
  <p>
    Official letters go to your registered office. These include Companies House reminders, HMRC penalties and court papers. One missed letter
    costs more than a year's difference between a £10 plan and a £20 one. So think about who handles the post. How quickly are you
    told it has arrived? Can you collect it? A provider you can phone and visit is worth a few pounds a month.
  </p>
  <p>
